  "projects": [
   {
    "title": "AWS Music Library Service",
    "description": "This Music Library Service is a serverless application designed to manage MP3 file uploads, retrievals, renaming, and deletions using AWS Lambda, S3, and API Gateway. The infrastructure is built with the AWS Cloud Development Kit (CDK), ensuring an Infrastructure-as-Code (IaC) approach for scalable deployment.\n\nThis is an ongoing personal project which I undertook primarily for the purposes of learning and becoming fluent in the AWS environment.  Feel free to take a look at the GitHub repo for this project, review the README overview, and browse the code.",
    "dateRange": {},
    "associatedWith": "Jan 2025 - Present"
   },
   {
    "title": "Sorting Game",
    "description": "After finalizing version 1 of my AWS Music Library Service project I thought I'd switch gears and tackle something a little lighter, so I went back to my coding bootcamp roots and threw this game together over the past week.  I always love when I get the chance to play around with fun sounds and effects as I feel like its the subtle animations that bring a game or a web app to life.  So here's my take on an easy to pick up, yet sneakily addictive, sorting game.  It's a mix of strategy and visual logic, all wrapped in a retro-computer aesthetic.  It has built-in difficulty scaling as you climb through the levels and several customizable gameplay, visual, and audio options in the settings menu.  And since the puzzles are randomly generated its infinitely playable.  Click or tap the \"?\" button to briefly review the rules if you need to, and have fun!  \n\nNote - While this game is fully optimized for desktop, it is also fully playable on mobile as well, though due to the way some phones handle audio playback in the browser, an occasional audio queue will be skipped or delayed. I've included a \"mute sounds\" button in the settings menu if you prefer to play without them.\n\nGitHub repo: https://github.com/ianhallWDJUN22/sorting-game",
    "dateRange": {},
    "associatedWith": "Mar 2025 - Mar 2025"
   }
